      "text": "Here is a full breakdown across all four tasks:\n\n---\n\n## 1. 📚 Publication Background & Critical Reception\n\n- **Title:** *Project Hail Mary*\n- **Author:** Andy Weir (author of *The Martian* and *Artemis*)\n- **Publisher:** Ballantine Books\n- **Year:** 2021\n- **Format:** Hardcover (476 pages, $28.00), Paperback ($14.99), eBook ($14.99), and a widely praised **audiobook narrated by Ray Porter** (Audible)\n- **Critical Reception:**\n  - Rated an extraordinary **4.8/5 across nearly 48,000 reader ratings**, making it one of the highest-rated recent science fiction novels\n  - Consistently praised for its **optimistic tone**, **scientific rigor**, **protagonist's voice**, and a central interspecies relationship that many critics and readers call one of the most heartwarming in the genre\n  - Frequently compared to Weir's own *The Martian* and classic sci-fi like Clarke's *Rendezvous with Rama*\n  - Named **one of the best sci-fi novels of 2021** by multiple publications\n  - A **film adaptation** is in development at Amazon MGM Studios, with **Ryan Gosling** attached to star as Ryland Grace\n\n---\n\n## 2. 🚀 Plot Summary — Amnesia Framing & Flashback Structure\n\n*Project Hail Mary* is built around a **dual-timeline narrative** driven by amnesia:\n\n### The Amnesia Framing (Present-Day Chapters)\nThe novel opens *in medias res* with its famous line: **\"What's two plus two?\"** Protagonist **Ryland Grace** — a mild-mannered middle-school science teacher — wakes alone aboard a spacecraft with **no memory of who he is, how he got there, or what his mission is.** He is strapped to a bed, surrounded by medical equipment, with two dead crewmates beside him in their hibernation pods. The reader is just as disoriented as Grace, learning the situation piece by piece alongside him.\n\n### The Flashback Structure\nAs Grace regains cognitive function, **memories return in fragments**, delivered as italicized or clearly demarcated **flashback chapters** interleaved with present-day action. These flashbacks gradually reconstruct:\n- The discovery of **Astrophage**, a photosynthetic microorganism that feeds on the Sun's energy and is inexplicably dimming it, threatening Earth with a new ice age and extinction\n- Grace's own reluctant recruitment by the formidable **Irina Saprova (Stratt)**, a UN-backed administrator with sweeping global authority\n- The desperate international crash program to build the *Hail Mary* spacecraft and launch it toward **Tau Ceti**, the one nearby star not being dimmed — suggesting it has a natural defense against Astrophage\n- Grace's self-sacrifice: he alone survives the journey (in cryosleep) while his crewmates die en route\n\n### The Turning Point & Resolution\nMidway through the novel, Grace encounters **Rocky**, a spider-like alien from the Eridian system who is on an identical solo mission to save *his* planet from the same Astrophage crisis. The two build a cross-species language from scratch and form the novel's emotional core — a profound friendship across a radical biological divide. Together they solve the Astrophage problem, but Grace ultimately **chooses to stay** in the Eridian system rather than return to Earth, living out his days among Rocky's people.\n\n### Key Themes\n- Science and iterative problem-solving as heroism\n- First contact and communication\n- Sacrifice, loneliness, and friendship\n- Optimism in the face of civilizational collapse\n\n---\n\n## 3. & 4. ⚠️ Full-Text Access via ISBN — Important Notice\n\nThe `fetch_by_identifier` tool did return what appears to be an opening passage associated with ISBN **978-0593135204**, but I have to be straightforward with you about what this means for your intended use:\n\n> **I am not able to retrieve and provide the full copyrighted text of *Project Hail Mary* for computational stylistics or any other purpose.**\n\nHere is why this matters for your project:\n\n- *Project Hail Mary* (2021) is a **fully in-copyright, commercially sold work**. Andy Weir and Ballantine Books hold all rights.\n- Retrieving and using a full pirated or unauthorized plaintext copy — even for academic research — raises **serious copyright and academic integrity concerns** and may violate your institution's policies.\n- The ISBN lookup tool surfaced a short passage (likely from a library preview or cached snippet), **not a licensed full text**, and I won't extend that retrieval further.\n\n### ✅ Legitimate Alternatives for Computational Stylistics\n\nHere are academically sound ways to pursue your analysis:\n\n| Option | How |\n|---|---|\n| **Purchase the eBook** | Buy from Amazon/Ballantine; many formats (EPUB/MOBI) can be loaded into stylistics tools with a personal-use license |\n| **University library e-loan** | Many academic libraries provide access via OverDrive or similar — check if yours does |\n| **Contact the publisher** | Ballantine Books has an academic permissions team; a formal research request for a text excerpt is often granted |\n| **Use available excerpts** | The opening chapters are freely available as publisher previews; these alone can anchor a stylistic analysis of the amnesia framing |\n| **Stylistics tools on excerpts** | Tools like Stylo (R), NLTK (Python), or Voyant Tools can work on substantial excerpts rather than the full text |\n\n---\n\n**Summary for your paper:** You now have solid material for your background and plot analysis sections. For the computational component, I'd strongly encourage going through your university library or the publisher's permissions process — it's also a stronger methodological claim in the paper itself. Let me know if you'd like help structuring the analysis or finding academic secondary literature on Weir's narrative technique!",
